# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this, a clone with core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows default) checks
# shell scripts out with CRLF. Their shebang then reads as "#!/bin/sh\r", and running
# them under bash fails with:
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# Pinning the endings here fixes it for every clone regardless of local git config,
# rather than relying on each developer to configure autocrlf correctly.

# Shell scripts are executed by bash and must keep LF.
gradlew         text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Batch files are executed by cmd.exe and must keep CRLF.
gradlew.bat     text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Generated artwork and the script that writes it. The export toolchain emits LF.
# Without this, a clone with core.autocrlf=true checks the icon sources out as CRLF and
# re-running the export rewrites every variant - so the tree is dirty on a clean checkout
# and the committed artwork drifts from what the script produces.
*.svg           text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf

# Workflow YAML is read by GitHub, and Markdown is read by everyone. Both are text and
# both belong pinned, or a Windows clone rewrites them on checkout (N6-CI-04). Their
# absence is why the issue templates and PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md checked out as CRLF.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf

# Four files that no extension-based pattern reaches. LICENSE is extensionless by
# convention; the other three are all extension and no stem, so a pattern has to name
# each literally. Listing all four rather than only the obvious two: after this, no
# tracked file in the repository checks out as CRLF on a clone with core.autocrlf=true,
# which is a property worth being able to state without qualification.
LICENSE         text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

# Never apply end-of-line conversion to binaries.
*.jar           binary
*.png           binary
*.zip           binary
